# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Office Space — what NEVER leaves your machine
#
# The rule: we ship the OFFICE (agents, skills, commands, settings).
# You keep the WORK (memory, projects, history, credentials).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# --- Credentials. Never, under any circumstances. ---
.credentials.json
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# --- Your memory is yours ---
memory/**
!memory/.gitkeep
!memory/README.md

# --- Your projects are yours (the convention is shipped, the content is not) ---
projects/**
!projects/.gitkeep
!projects/README.md
!projects/_TEMPLATE/
!projects/_TEMPLATE/**

# --- Claude Code local state ---
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/history/
.claude/todos/
.claude/shell-snapshots/
.claude/statsig/
.claude/projects/
.claude/.office-space/

# --- Office Space runtime state (session registry, floor cache) ---
.office-space/

# --- Art you licensed yourself ---
# Office Space ships one theme: the characters it draws in code. If you buy a commercial
# pixel-art pack, it goes here — and it stays here. Every pack we looked at permits
# commercial use and forbids redistribution, so committing one would breach the licence you
# just paid for. See docs/themes.md.
assets/themes/**
!assets/themes/README.md

# --- Node / build ---
# No trailing slash, deliberately.
#
# `node_modules/` matches a directory and does NOT match a **symlink** named node_modules — git
# stages it, and what goes in is an absolute path that means nothing on anybody else's machine.
#
# That is not hypothetical here: `scripts/preflight.mjs` tells an agent working in a worktree to
# create exactly that symlink, because a fresh install per worktree costs minutes and a network.
# So the documented sequence was: run preflight, follow its fix, `git add -A`, commit a symlink
# to /Users/somebody/... Caught by the second session checking `git status` after staging rather
# than assuming, on the night both were doing it.
node_modules
dist/
out/
# electron-builder's output. `dist/` above is the compiled source; this is the 286MB packaged
# application, and it appeared the first time anybody ran `npm run package` — which is also the
# first time anybody could have noticed it was missing from this list. `git add -A` in a repo
# that has been packaged would otherwise commit an entire Electron runtime.
release/
*.log
.DS_Store

# --- Manual checks that spend real money to run ---
scratch/

# --- The reference workspace is yours, not ours ---
# A copy of a real, working `.claude/` workspace, kept here so the office can be built
# against something verified rather than something imagined. It carries knowledge of
# projects that are not this one, so it is read here and never shipped.
reference/

# --- Agent worktrees ---
# Here rather than in `.git/info/exclude`, which is where this rule used to live alone.
#
# `info/exclude` is per-checkout and uncommitted: it is one machine's config, it arrives in no
# clone, and — the part that bit — **git does not apply it inside a linked worktree**. Committed
# rules in this file do. So the 374 MB of agent checkouts `docs/the-sweep.md` measured were
# ignored on exactly one working tree on exactly one machine, and nowhere else. In a fresh clone
# `git add -A` would have staged all of it, which is the accident `release/` above is here to
# prevent and the same one this line prevents.
#
# It also makes the question answerable by a test. `repo-health.test.ts` asks git whether a
# documented path is an ignored runtime artifact or a repo file it should insist exists; while
# the rule lived only in `info/exclude`, git gave a different answer per checkout and the test
# graded the machine rather than the commit.
#
# No trailing slash, for the second time in this file and for a second reason. `worktrees/`
# matches only something git can see is a directory, so it says nothing about a path that is not
# on this disk — which is the whole case here: in a fresh clone and in every worktree the
# directory is absent, and `git check-ignore .claude/worktrees` came back "not ignored" with the
# slash on. Without it the rule is about the name, so git answers the same everywhere.
.claude/worktrees
graphify-out/*
!graphify-out/GRAPH_REPORT.md
!graphify-out/.graphify_labels.json
